      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 155,
      "outputTokens": 1213,
      "costUsd": 0.010994499999999999,
      "pronunciationText": "Here's why. Call the graph f, so f of t is three minus t. Move the end from x to a little further, x plus h. A gains one thin strip, with width h. Its height is about f of x, so A grows by about f of x times h. Divide by h, and the gradient of the chord on ay is about f of x. That's the gradient from first principles. At x equals one, with h equal to a half, the chord's gradient is one point seven five. With h equal to a tenth, it's one point nine five. As h shrinks, the strip's top gets flatter, because the curve is continuous. So the gradient becomes exactly f of x."
    },
