      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 122,
      "outputTokens": 979,
      "costUsd": 0.008872,
      "pronunciationText": "So differentiating the accumulated area gives back the height. In words, the area grows at a rate equal to the height at its moving edge. If f is a rate, the amount built up so far grows at that rate. For the car park, the count of extra cars grows at the net rate cars come in. Check it here. The trapezium from zero to x has heights three and three minus x, and width x. So A of x is three x, minus a half x squared. Its derivative is three minus x, the graph we started with. So ay is a primitive of f."
    },
