      "pronunciationText": "Is this only ay trick for straight lines? Try a curve. Shade under t squared, from zero up to x. This time we can't use simple shapes, so a graphing application adds up very thin strips for us, and plots A of x as x moves. Watch the gradient of A as a point moves along. It traces x squared, as closely as the strips allow. Try it in a graphing app: move x and watch. And a primitive of x squared is x cubed over three, which matches the plotted curve. The edge-strip reason didn't need a straight line. It only needed a continuous curve."
