      "outputTokens": 1088,
      "costUsd": 0.009865500000000001,
      "pronunciationText": "Now the big result. Take any primitive F of f. Any two primitives differ by a constant, so F of x equals A of x, plus C. At the start, A of ay is zero, because no strips have been added yet. So F of b, minus F of ay, is A of b plus C, minus C. The constants cancel, and that leaves A of b, the integral from a to b. That's the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus. For a continuous f, the integral from a to b of f of x, d x, equals F of b minus F of ay, for any primitive F. We write it with square brackets: F of x, from a to b."
